    {
      "heading": "Letter",
      "section_kind": "letter_body",
      "body": "Dear Ms Doe,\n\nIn its letter SSM-2026-0301 of 7 July 2026, the ECB asked significant institutions to assess the effect of AI-enabled cybersecurity threats without delay, and to send their Joint Supervisory Team, by 31 October 2026, an action plan with concrete measures, the resources allocated to them, clear roles and responsibilities and implementation dates. Northwind Bank AG (the Bank) submits its action plan with this letter, in Annex 1.\n\n### Approval and resources\n\nThe Management Board approved the action plan on 21 October 2026, after the Risk Committee of the Supervisory Board reviewed it on 16 October 2026. The Management Board assessed whether the Bank's ICT budget, staffing, tooling and change capacity are sufficient to carry it out, and allocated an additional EUR 4.2 million for 2027 and eight full-time positions in vulnerability management and security operations. The ICT risk tolerance framework now carries a patching-latency metric for internet-facing assets, with a tolerance of seven days for critical vulnerabilities.\n\n### Short-term measures\n\nThe plan gives priority to the three short-term areas the ECB named. Within them it puts perimeter technologies and internet-facing assets first, including third-party software and open-source components. The most significant measures are:\n\n- a complete inventory of internet-facing assets and their software components, by 31 December 2026;\n- risk-based scanning of all internet-facing assets daily, from 30 November 2026;\n- a review of the patching terms in the contracts with the Bank's eleven critical ICT service providers, by 31 March 2027.\n\n### Open supervisory findings\n\nTwo findings on ICT and security risk remain open: finding 4 of the 2024 on-site inspection on access management, which will be closed by 31 January 2027, and finding 2 of the 2024 cyber resilience stress test on backup restoration, which will be closed by 31 March 2027. Both are part of the plan.\n\nThe Bank will report progress against the plan to the Joint Supervisory Team quarterly, starting with the fourth quarter of 2026, and will inform it without delay if any date in the plan is at risk.\n\nFor any question on this letter or the action plan, please contact Alex Muster, Head of Regulatory Affairs (alex.muster@northwind-bank.example).\n\nYours sincerely,",
